Chapter 10, Section 15: "Together to Hell"

—The moment the spreading hellfire swallowed Natsuki Subaru, Beatrice couldn't believe her eyes.

"Why...!?"

The deployed E・M・T was a forbidden technique—a cheat magic that, under different circumstances, would undoubtedly have been classified as a forbidden art. It manipulated magical interference generated within its field, primarily by altering the composition of magic formulas and forcibly inserting commands to dismantle and dissolve them.

Of course, if someone were to see through the mechanism of E・M・T and construct a formula designed to keep functioning even as its magical structure was unraveled, it could potentially be broken. But pulling off such a feat—something as absurd as replacing a single part in a precisely crafted magical device with something completely different and having it still work the same way—would be impossible for anyone except an irregular like Roswaal or the Witch of Greed—her mother.

Aside from such anomalies, no one should be able to activate magic.

Yet—

"How in the world did you break through it?"

"—Break through? That's making it sound grand. I just racked my brain and put my body on the line."

"—"

As Beatrice posed her question, narrowing her eyes, the answer came from Tigar Rauleon, who was leaning half of his body against the wall, having raised himself upright.

Amid the fierce battle, the young man who should have been utterly wrecked after being kicked into the wall by the rampaging possessor of the "Dragon Eye"—Crusch—let out a profoundly exhausted sigh. Tigar held his beloved shamshir in one hand, while the other was pressed against his side. Beatrice shuddered when she saw that the clothing around his flank, where his hand rested, was scorched, the skin beneath charred black.

That was not a wound inflicted by Crusch's pursuit. And that wound—was exactly—

"...What a horrifying thought process you've got there."

"I only did what was necessary, Lady Beatrice. As the scripture says: 'While the dragon circles the heavens, let man till the earth. He who lets his allotted time rot in slumber shall not even tread upon the dragon's shadow when the harvest season arrives.'"

Slowly shaking his head, Tigar recited a passage from the Holy Dragon Church's scripture, reminiscent of something Felt might say. However, whether the fact that he had read the scripture so deeply as to recite it from memory truly demonstrated the decisiveness of his choice—Beatrice could not easily nod in agreement.

The scorched wound on Tigar's side—it was caused by the same flames that had swallowed Subaru and Crusch. But those flames had burned him not from the outside, but from within.

The effect of E・M・T was to dismantle the composition of any magic activated within its field.

Tigar had forcefully broken through this "within the field" condition.

And his method was—

"—Magic generated inside my own body isn't affected by this forbidden technique. ...I wasn't entirely certain, but it seems I was right."

"—"

"I noticed it in the middle of the fight. I thought, 'Even though we can't use magic right now, the effects of my Flow Technique are still fully in effect.' And so—"

"So does that mean slashing your own belly open and using magic with the resolve to have your insides burned through are easy choices? Both of them? ...Only a person driven by something beyond a sense of duty—whose heart is captive to something more—could do that, don't you think?"

"I'm a bit put out that it sounds like you're calling me insane. ...Not that I can blame you."

Though his voice was heavy with exhaustion, his reply was matter-of-fact. Hearing it, Beatrice felt an unsettling chill run through her, as if her non-existent liver had gone cold.

To Beatrice, the Tigar she knew was a person she had only been acquainted with for one or two days since their meeting at a church of the Holy Dragon Church—an exceedingly brief period. Yet even in that short time, she had genuinely felt a fondness for his easy rapport with Subaru and his witty, pleasant demeanor.

That impression refused to align with the Tigar standing before her now. This machine-like decision—disregarding his own well-being entirely to accomplish a necessary task—was the complete opposite of the person she had encountered until now.

What on earth could drive him to such lengths?

A devotion beyond a sense of duty, an obsession that defied all reason—Beatrice knew a person who moved with such resolve. Tigar's fixation was of the same nature as Roswaal's.

A man who, over four hundred years, had derailed countless fates and yet still sought to reach the pinnacle of a demon's single-minded purpose. Was Tigar headed toward the same destination as Roswaal?

"I'm just doing what's necessary. ...To take back my life."

In response to Beatrice's silent suspicion, Tigar shrugged slightly and answered. Then he looked toward the back of the room, where the flames roared intensely.

"Frankly, to me, your reaction, Lady Beatrice, is more unexpected—or rather, surprising. Honestly, I was prepared for you to blast me with magic the moment I did that. After all, what I did... was what it was."

"...And you're taking it to mean that it wasn't a split-second misjudgment made in desperation, correct?"

"Yes, that's right. —Duke Crusch Karsten repeatedly refused our demands for surrender. I even gave a final warning, and she cut it down with her blade."

"—"

"After killing Lord Macmahon, she turned her blade on us too. She wasn't in her right mind. So I made a judgment call on the spot to eliminate the threat as thoroughly as possible. ...Is what I'm saying wrong?"

With a cold lack of emotion plastered onto his handsome, dandy features, Tigar asked Beatrice to judge the right or wrong of his actions.

There was no face of a martyr regretting his deeds and seeking forgiveness—only the matter-of-fact expression of an executioner confirming where responsibility lay for his bloodstained hands.

Within Beatrice, her initial impression of Tigar was now forever beyond recovery.

"What you're saying is... not wrong, I suppose."

Facing Tigar directly, Beatrice narrowed her round eyes and nodded.

Indeed, that was the case. Ever since Beatrice and the others had witnessed the tragedy at Macmahon Manor and encountered the perpetrator, Crusch, Tigar's actions had not been incorrect. To the fullest extent, he had tried to follow Subaru's intentions and cooperate in subduing Crusch. And it was indisputably Crusch herself who had, at every turn, rejected those decisions.

To protect himself from that dangerous individual, Tigar had the right to make a ruthless decision. Even if that decision involved dragging Subaru—their ally—into the fray, using that as a reason to condemn him without question would not be permissible on moral grounds.

Which was why—

"Betty isn't so unreasonable as to selfishly condemn you."

"...I see. Then—"

"—However."

With that, Beatrice cut off Tigar's words.

For an instant, what flickered in Tigar's golden eyes was not surprise, but something cold and ruthless. Pushing aside her feelings about that to a corner of her mind, Beatrice continued.

Tigar's words and deeds were justified. And yet—

"—Betty is going to side with the one who saved Subaru."

In that instant—the searing intensity of the flames she faced was cleaved in two from the other side.

"—"

The silently slashed flames, flickering and wavering, were split apart. Standing calmly in the space opened in their center was the possessor of the "Dragon Eye"—Crusch Karsten, holding an unconscious Subaru in her left hand while her right hand had delivered the knife-hand strike that cut the fire.

"Heat... no, the flow of power itself was cut. So this is 'Sword of Death'."

Touching the eyelid of her "Dragon Eye" with the right hand that had launched the strike, Crusch murmured with an air of comprehension.

While marveling at the fact that she had swept away that hellfire with a single swing of her arm, Beatrice checked on Subaru, cradled in Crusch's arm. A wave of relief even greater than her astonishment washed over her.

—The reason Beatrice hadn't panicked the moment Subaru was swallowed by the flames was that the connection she shared with him as her contractor had transmitted no response indicating any grave danger.

That fact, along with what she had seen at the very last moment—Crusch pulling Subaru down to protect him from the onrushing flames and leaping before the fire herself—had solidified Beatrice's decision.

Beatrice would side with Crusch Karsten.

"At the very least, Betty will take the role of listening to her story. The finer judgments will come after that."

"—That's not a wise decision, Lady Beatrice. Just because your contractor Subaru was saved, you shouldn't let your judgment be clouded. The Duke is dangerous."

"Don't treat Betty like some reckless, thoughtless spirit. ...Though, come to think of it, if Betty were a rash and indiscriminate spirit, then siding with the girl who saved Subaru would be only natural, wouldn't it?"

"Is this really the time for splitting hairs!? People will soon notice this fire and come running. It'll be too late to regret picking the wrong side. What will happen to Subaru?!"

"Those words don't carry much weight coming from the one who almost burned Subaru along with her. But you're right, there really doesn't seem to be much time. So—"

As Beatrice spoke, she slowly raised her right hand, and Tigar held his breath at her gesture.

It wasn't the preparation for magic. It was the opposite. Tigar's golden eyes narrowed sharply at Beatrice's intention as she prepared to snap her fingers. He pointed his shamshir toward her.

"Stop. Don't do it. I really won't be able to protect you."

"Long ago, Betty regretted merely watching a friend depart, doing nothing but crying pathetically. —She won't make the same mistake twice."

Even when waves of turmoil crash down like a raging torrent, Beatrice will, as long as she is permitted, make her own decision and choose her path.

Just as losing a friend after simply being swept along taught her—she will never again cower before something precious.

For that reason—

"—In this place, Betty stands with you, Crusch Karsten!"

With a snap of her fingers, Beatrice used that as the signal to release it. —E・M・T.

Immediately, those with keen senses would have discerned the invisible field enveloping the office vanishing into thin air.

Neither Tigar nor the owner of the "Dragon Eye" were exceptions to this—

"—Hundred-Man Single Stroke."

An explosive burst of wind erupted, shaking the entire Macmahon Manor, centered on the office.



