"—And after shaking off that man, we escaped into the sewers. I wanted to flee much farther, but I couldn't afford to be reckless while Subaru was taking his nap."

Listening to Beatrice, who gently nestled beside him and supported his body, Subaru sorted through the information in his muddled head and somehow managed to mutter, "...I see."

Honestly, with so much having happened, he felt pathetic for being the only one to drop out of consciousness so early. But such self-reproach would have to wait for later.

"First... Bea-tan, not leaving Crusch-san's side—that was a great call. If we'd gotten separated in that situation, it would've been regret beyond regret."

"Of course, it's only natural to give the utmost consideration to my partner's feelings. Betty was absolutely certain that Subaru would do the same."

"Even so, from an outside perspective it was a reckless choice, so I can't exactly just go, 'Hell yeah!' without reservation..."

Given the circumstances, he couldn't help but let out a wry smile at Beatrice's proud expression.

But that brief moment of loosened lips was the only part of their conversation that allowed it. —His eyes fell on the burn on his arm, throbbing with a dull, persistent pain, and he bit his lip.

"That bastard Tigar... did this?"

"Without a doubt. Whether his claim of being unable to use magic during the fight was a bluff, or perhaps... no, never mind."

"What? If there's something bothering you, it's bothering me."

"I simply decided it's not something to deliberate on now, with so many worries already piled up. Anyway, that man used magic fully prepared to catch us as collateral. Betty's impression of him has crashed straight into the ground."

"...Of course you'd feel that way, since you love me so much."

Beatrice's anger, with her raised eyebrows and puffed cheeks, left Subaru's own feelings exceedingly complicated.

Of course, he had no intention of glossing over an attack that could have killed him. None. But, it had been a desperate opening found on the razor's edge between life and death, while being utterly overwhelmed by Crusch. He couldn't condemn Tigar's split-second decision in the heat of battle out of hand.

At that point, Beatrice's gaze on Subaru sharpened.

"Subaru? Don't tell me..."

"No, I'm not gonna gloss it over. I fully intend to demand an apology for the pain of this burn. That is absolutely non-negotiable. Obviously, right?"

"—"

"Huh? Why are you looking at me with even more exasperation?! I said I'm gonna make him apologize!"

Subaru's confusion only deepened at Beatrice's half-lidded stare following his answer.

Both sides had their faults, and he was judging the other party's error to be somewhat larger. He felt he was taking a pretty resolute stance by his standards.

Besides—

"At the very least, the friction between us and Tigar is largely due to a misunderstanding. If we can talk it out properly somewhere, that misunderstanding should be cleared up—"

"—Regrettably, I have no intention of allowing you to do so."

As Subaru carefully chose his words, trying somehow to persuade Beatrice, the one who put a halt to his speech was not Beatrice, her brow furrowed, but Crusch, who was walking ahead of them.

Leading the way through the dim sewer by the light of a Lagmite ore lamp, she had taken clear issue with the conversation between Subaru and Beatrice—and she made that perfectly clear by drawing her short sword and pointing its tip at Subaru.

"...C-Crusch-san?"

"I will not permit contact with Tigar Rauleon. Aside from the fact that we have crossed blades, that man is one of those now commanding the pursuers closing in on us. If you carelessly go to meet him, you will only end up in shackles."

"But that's... I mean, from Tigar's position, it can't be helped, or rather, given the situation..."

"—"

"—"

Facing Crusch, her left eye covered by an eyepatch, with only her amber single eye, Subaru hesitated greatly over his next choice of words.

The atrocity at Macmahon Manor, and now the fact that she was pointing her short sword at him here. —Speaking with the current Crusch required the utmost caution. So much so that a careless remark, inadvertently provoking her, might truly cause him to stain her hands with blood this time.

With such thoughts, the staring contest between them continued for a while—

"...Astonishingly, I do not feel the wind of deceit blowing from you."

Saying that, it was Crusch who lowered her short sword first.

At her words, Subaru's eyes widened. "Huh?" But Crusch returned her short sword to the sheath on her belt and continued,

"No wind of falsehood blows from you. That has been constant from the moment we encountered you at Microtov Macmahon's estate, all the way until now. That is why I permitted Beatrice to accompany us."

"—In the end... in the end, Crusch-san, what do you want to do with us?"

Exchanging a brief glance with Beatrice at his side, Subaru posed the question directly to Crusch.

The wind of falsehood—as the bearer of the Divine Protection of Wind Reading, Crusch could perceive the thoughts and intentions of others as wind, effectively functioning as a kind of lie detector.

When Subaru first met her, the deception in his heart had been seen through, making him keenly aware of his own twisted nature. —Now, that very ability was the basis for her words and actions.

Trusting Subaru was also a result of the Divine Protection of Wind Reading.

If so, then was cutting down Microtov, and the thirteen people in that mansion, also a result of that Divine Protection?

"Tell me, Crusch-san. What... no, the main thing is about you, Crusch-san. What are you trying to do that's led you to this?"

"—. What I wish to do... no, what I must do, is to save this Kingdom of Lugunica from the hands of traitors. That will likely be the final act I can perform for the kingdom."

"Traitors... and, 'final act'...?"

"Would you insist otherwise? Having succumbed to the malice of the Witch's Cult, bedridden and having wasted a long time in vain. On top of that, in a manner directly opposite to my public pledge to abolish the dragon's rule, I was raised up by the hands of the Holy Dragon Church. Do you truly think there is any chance of victory left for me?"

"That's...!"

"The wind of understanding blows. —You are an honest man, you."

Lowering her eyes slightly, Crusch etched a self-deprecating smile onto her lips.

Through the eyes of her faint, weak smile, she must have seen it, by means of her Divine Protection. Subaru's powerlessness—unable to deny her words, unable to conjure up any reliable breakthrough plan.

Being called honest for that was nothing to be pleased about.

"Enough of this, I say. Betty won't tolerate you acting like a tragic heroine and snapping at Subaru any longer."

"Beatrice..."

"And Subaru, you're Subaru. What are you getting depressed over something that isn't your responsibility? At this rate, you'll start blaming yourself even when a child falls over on the roadside. If that happens, you'll be so busy you won't have any time to adore Betty. I lodge a vehement protest."

With her hands on her hips, Beatrice revealed an anger so out of place. Realizing it was her own way of showing consideration—trying to minimize the issue between Subaru and Crusch—a sigh escaped him.

Crusch, too, nodded with a solemn expression. "You're right, Beatrice."

"This is not the time to obsess over trivial grievances. The more time we give the opposing side, the more disadvantageous it becomes for us. Therefore, Natsuki Subaru, I wish to ask for your cooperation—no, yours and Beatrice's."

"...Cooperation?"

"Indeed. At this moment, the kingdom is in the midst of a crisis of national survival, due to the machinations of numerous traitors who have infiltrated even its very core. It is precisely in this situation that I want to borrow your strength—the power of Natsuki Subaru, who cooperated with our house and made immense contributions to the subjugation of the White Whale."

"—"

Faced with Crusch, who had sheathed her short sword and extended her now-empty hand toward him as she spoke, Subaru held his breath involuntarily and stared directly at her.

What startled Subaru was not just the request Crusch had voiced. Of course, that request itself greatly astonished him, but what surprised him even more was that she had spoken of the great decisive battle against the White Whale—once waged in the past—as something that had certainly happened to her.

Taking that in, Subaru finally voiced the question that had been continuously lodged in his heart from the moment they encountered Crusch at Macmahon Manor, all the way up to now.

That question—

"Let me ask you one thing. —Did your 'memories' return, Crusch-san?"

"—. Of course. Do you see in me now any trace of that detestable, feeble Crusch Karsten from when I lacked my 'memories'? That question, if anything, is even insulting."

"—"

Hearing Crusch clearly state the restoration of her "memories," Subaru's cheeks stiffened.

The shock it brought was complex, a mixture of joy and indignation. The fact that Crusch's "memories" had returned was, in itself, something to be happy about from the bottom of his heart. But that did not justify denigrating the Crusch of the time without her "memories" so maliciously.

"A wind of dissatisfaction. Surely you don't mean to say you would support the 'me' without memories?"

"It's not that simple of a matter, is it? The Crusch-san with her 'memories' now, and the one without back then—to me, both were Crusch-san..."

"—No, they are not. A Crusch Karsten without her 'memories' could never be Crusch Karsten. That is why...!"

"—"

"...That is why Ferris, too, gave up on Crusch Karsten."

"Wha—!"

Watching Crusch tremble, her back teeth grinding, Subaru was about to assert firmly, "That's the one thing you're wrong about!"

He had thought the same thing not just in this moment, but even before they started moving.

Crusch misunderstood the meaning of Ferris's decision, and the depth of his resolve.

It was true that Ferris had broken Crusch's oath—the oath for the late royal family.

Even Subaru, having only heard the story, could imagine well enough how precious that was to Crusch. But that same weight applied to Ferris as well.

Ferris, too, had loved the late Fourth Prince. And yet, he had made his choice.

Love for the living, over love for the dead. —Subaru refused to see that denied.

Much less the tragedy of two people whose feelings were directed at each other, failing to share them due to a simple misunderstanding—

"—It appears this is as far as we can draw out our decision."

However, before Subaru could give vent to that surge of emotion, the situation refused to allow any more delay.

Crusch's murmur, as she looked behind them, deeper into the sewer passages they had traversed, was explained fully by the voices and footfalls echoing from a considerable distance away. —Pursuers.

A company of pursuers, searching for Crusch, had descended into the sewers, coming for Subaru and the others.

"If we could just talk to them properly..."

"You should already understand. You are already seen as having colluded with me. The sword taken from you by my strange technique was also left behind at the scene. My involvement would be clear if compared with the wounds on the corpses. Furthermore—"

"Furthermore? What else? Is there even worse news than this?"

"Despite the presence of Tigar Rauleon, who knew your intentions, you were still identified as being involved in the murder of Microtov Macmahon. That man must have reported it so."

"—"

"After that, the choice is yours. If you deem me unworthy of trust, you may seek protection from the pursuers coming from behind. —Farewell."

Having said her piece without restraint, and making it clear she had no intention of further conversation, Crusch turned her back, tossed the lamp into the sewer water, and dashed off swiftly.

As even the faint light of the sinking source failed to reach her retreating back, only her fading footsteps pressed Subaru for a choice, like a countdown.

To reject Crusch's argument, join up with the soldiers pursuing them, spill everything, and seek protection. Or to chase after the back of Crusch, disappearing into darkness to fight a vast conspiracy all alone. One or the other.

"Damn it! I want to believe! I want to believe you, but is there even really such a conspiracy in the first place...?!"

Her state of mind—misinterpreting Ferris's true intentions, not even hesitating to point a blade even at Subaru. In addition to impulsive emotional reactions, Crusch's emotional state was unstable.

At worst, everything—including the slaying of Microtov—could be feared to be nothing more than a paranoid rampage, devoid of any legitimacy.

In such a situation, what was the right thing to do—?

"Subaru, calm down and listen. No matter which you choose, no matter what you side with, Betty is Subaru's ally and will always be at his side."

"Bea-tan..."

"I absolutely refuse to be separated like we were in the Empire. If Subaru isn't somewhere I can keep my eyes on him, I'll be so worried I won't be able to breathe."

So said Beatrice to Subaru, lost and uncertain of the right answer—telling him that whatever he chose, she would make it the right answer. No, that they would make it the right answer together.

His hesitation cleared. That's right. Beatrice was absolutely right.

Even if he made a choice, wasn't struggling to make it the right answer Natsuki Subaru's most practiced way of fighting?

"I can't leave Crusch-san alone. You're coming with me to hell, Beatrice!"

"If I'm with Subaru, I wouldn't mind even being sealed for four hundred years!"

"I would mind, so don't say scary things like that, please!?"

As he spoke, Subaru scooped up his reliable, beloved partner, and chasing after the fading footsteps, he plunged into the darkness at full speed.

Whether it was right or wrong, he couldn't abandon Crusch.

Whether to stop her, or to help her—he must not part from Crusch.

"Ah, damn, my arm hurts!"

The burn on his arm throbbed persistently, as if rebuking him for his foolishness.

Bearing that pain, even more than lamenting his situation—swept up and driven onward at a dizzying pace—Subaru worried for Emilia and the others, once they heard about this.

Causing trouble was one thing, but the pain of causing them worry was greater still.

To Emilia, Rem, Ram, Petra, Meili, Garfiel—to everyone. So, biting back the ache in his mind and body, Subaru desperately strained his eyes into the dim darkness and,

"Somehow, pull through for me, Otto!"

—shouted, flinging this sudden predicament onto his absent friend as he ran.





